{"title":"K Th Symbol In Grammar","difficulty":"Medium","statement":"We build a table of n rows (1-indexed). We start by writing 0 in the 1st row. Now in every subsequent row, we look at the previous row and replace each occurrence of 0 with 01, and each occurrence of 1 with 10. For example, for n = 3, the 1st row is 0, the 2nd row is 01, and the 3rd row is 0110. Given two integer n and k, return the kth (1-indexed) symbol in the nth row of a table of n rows.\n\n## Examples\n\n### Example 1\n\n```text\nInput: n = 1, k = 1\nOutput: 0\n```\n\nExplanation: row 1: 0\n\n### Example 2\n\n```text\nInput: n = 2, k = 1\nOutput: 0\n```\n\nExplanation: row 1: 0 row 2: 01\n\n### Example 3\n\n```text\nInput: n = 2, k = 2\nOutput: 1\n```\n\nExplanation: row 1: 0 row 2: 01\n\n## Constraints\n\n- 1 <= n <= 30\n- 1 <= k <= 2^(n-1)\n\nRows are indexed from 1 and columns from 1. Row 1 is [0]; each 0 produces 01 and each 1 produces 10. Return the symbol at column k. The row has 2^(n-1) symbols.","starterCode":"class Solution:\n    def kthGrammar(self, n: int, k: int) -> int:\n        pass\n","entryPoint":"Solution().kthGrammar","parameters":[{"name":"n","type":"int"},{"name":"k","type":"int"}],"returnType":"int","tests":[{"input":[1,1],"expected":0},{"input":[10,102],"expected":0},{"input":[10,118],"expected":1},{"input":[10,13],"expected":0},{"input":[10,138],"expected":1},{"input":[10,14],"expected":1},{"input":[10,144],"expected":1},{"input":[10,16],"expected":0},{"input":[10,176],"expected":0},{"input":[10,18],"expected":0},{"input":[10,182],"expected":1},{"input":[10,184],"expected":0},{"input":[10,189],"expected":1},{"input":[10,2],"expected":1},{"input":[10,261],"expected":0},{"input":[10,296],"expected":1},{"input":[10,301],"expected":0},{"input":[10,304],"expected":0},{"input":[10,344],"expected":0},{"input":[10,349],"expected":1},{"input":[10,364],"expected":0},{"input":[10,378],"expected":0},{"input":[10,382],"expected":1},{"input":[10,394],"expected":0},{"input":[10,413],"e